Abstract 
The aim of this bachelor thesis is the development and implementation of an 
autonomous surveillance drone system capable of independently monitoring defined areas, 
detecting potential objects, and processing relevant data in real time. For this purpose, a 
quadcopter was constructed using a modular hardware architecture consisting of a Speedybee 
F405 V4 flight controller, a Raspberry Pi as companion computer, a GPS module, and a camera 
unit. 
The flight control is managed through the open-source ArduPilot framework, while 
communication between the components is established via the MAVLink protocol. For object 
detection, the lightweight neural network YOLOv4 Tiny was implemented on the Raspberry 
Pi, enabling real-time recognition of people and vehicles. Detected data is stored locally using 
an SQLite database and can be accessed via a ground control station. 
Multiple flight tests were conducted to validate the system. The results demonstrated 
that the developed prototype is capable of reliable waypoint navigation while providing 
accurate and fast object detection. 
This work contributes to the advancement of cost-effective autonomous UAV solutions, 
which can be applied in fields such as security monitoring, disaster response, and environmental 
observation. 
